<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mbunda people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mbunda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mbunda are a Bantu people of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. Groups of them migrated east into Barotseland from the late eighteenth century, during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litunga">Litunga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mulambwa">Mulambwa</a>, who welcomed them; they were valued as warriors and diviners and helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> against their enemies. Many more arrived in the colonial period, and they now form a large community in Western Province of Zambia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Mbunda, or Vambunda, speak Mbunda and comprise several subgroups with dialects of their own, among them the Mbunda Mathzi, Yauma, Nkangala, Mbalango, Sango, Shamuka and Ndundu; they are often counted among the Ganguela peoples. Their oral tradition names Mwene Nkuungu as the first monarch of the Mbunda Kingdom and tells of a settlement on the Mithimoyi, a tributary of the Luena, followed by expansion south into what became known as Mbundaland, with its capital at Lumbala Nguimbo.</p><p>Tradition also recounts wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chokwe">Chokwe</a>, set off when the 20th monarch, Mwene Katavola II Musangu, sought to marry a Chokwe slave and was killed, and with the Luvale, whose leader Masambo was shot by King Mwene Mbandu I Lyondthzi Kapova in the Lunjweva area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Some Mbunda reached Barotseland at the end of the 18th century. The Litunga Mulambwa prized them as fighters, and after they turned back a Luvale invasion he made a treaty with their leader Mwene Chitengi Chiyengele and confirmed him as Senior Chief of the Mbunda, who gained a place on the Barotse National Council. They fought beside the Aluyi against the Makololo in 1830, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> in the 1880s and the Kaonde. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, the Portuguese abducted Mbandu I early in the 20th century; lacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, the Mbunda were defeated and a second migration followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angolan-war-of-independence">Angolan War of Independence</a> and the civil war of 1975 to 2002 drove further waves of refugees into western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Rule in the kingdom passed through the central matrilineal line, and the king's decisions were not questioned, though local rule was decentralised. Tradition holds that a prince consort, Mukwetunga Kamenga, brought back the Mukanda circumcision rite from the Mbwela, and that because it was for men only, Queen Vamwene Mukenge was the last woman to reign. In Mukanda, boys spend three to six months at a bush camp, and the Makishi masquerade is performed. The Mbunda work iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, fish, carve wood, weave baskets, make bark-cloth and grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> as their staple. Most follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, Catholic or Protestant; some honour Njambi as creator through the ancestors. The Mbunda Bible was launched in 2008 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and 2009 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mbunda people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom drew on the different environments around it through tribute: subject peoples such as the Nkoya, Toka-Leya, Subiya and <b>Mbunda</b> sent iron hoes, canoes, honey, skins, grain and ivory to the capital, while commoners owed the king periods of labour, building mounds and digging drainage canals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
